WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.239 --> 00:00:03.388
Creating and maintaining 
a data science environment

00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:05.180
is no easy task.

00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.205
&gt;From installing libraries
to updating runtimes,

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:12.774
it can be a real drain
on both your time and productivity.

00:00:12.774 --> 00:00:17.398
What if I told you there was a tool
that could make all of that go away

00:00:17.398 --> 00:00:20.426
so you can focus on the data science
and machine learning?

00:00:20.426 --> 00:00:22.507
What might such a tool look like?

00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:24.286
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:27.360
♪ (music) ♪

00:00:32.648 --> 00:00:34.494
Welcome to AI Adventures,

00:00:34.494 --> 00:00:38.238
where we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:40.777
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:45.045
we're going to take a look at how Colab 
can help you learn data science

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:47.904
and experiment 
with your machine learning models.

00:00:48.803 --> 00:00:51.309
Colaboratory, or Colab for short,

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:54.920
is a tool for machine learning 
education and research.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.227
In a nutshell, it's basically 
a Jupyter Notebook environment

00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:00.384
that requires no setup to use,

00:01:00.384 --> 00:01:02.727
and I mean zero setup.

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:06.283
Since it runs in the browser,
there's no local installation,

00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:10.216
no updating of libraries,
no managing of Python environments.

00:01:10.216 --> 00:01:13.565
Colab comes with lots 
of Python libraries preinstalled

00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:15.833
so <i>you</i> can get right to work.

00:01:16.588 --> 00:01:19.140
It also saves directly to Google Drive

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.914
so you can keep your work up to date

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:24.261
without needing 
to worry about it getting lost.

00:01:24.261 --> 00:01:28.982
This means you get sharing and comments
just like in Google Docs.

00:01:28.982 --> 00:01:32.877
This totally changes 
the collaboration options

00:01:32.877 --> 00:01:34.993
when it comes to data science.

00:01:35.445 --> 00:01:40.032
Speaking of collaboration,
Colab also has revision history

00:01:40.032 --> 00:01:42.849
just like the rest 
of your Google Drive files.

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:45.937
So you can open
an old version, download it,

00:01:45.937 --> 00:01:48.942
or just revert to that version 
entirely if you want.

00:01:49.373 --> 00:01:52.708
Of course, I've been leaving out 
one of the top features of Colab

00:01:52.708 --> 00:01:54.972
that users absolutely love.

00:01:54.972 --> 00:01:57.896
You can use a free GPU in Colab.

00:01:57.896 --> 00:01:59.887
No credit card, no signups.

00:01:59.887 --> 00:02:04.305
Just go on over and change at runtime
to include a GPU accelerator.

00:02:04.305 --> 00:02:06.423
While you're there, you might also notice

00:02:06.423 --> 00:02:10.294
that you can attach a TPU 
instead of a GPU.

00:02:10.294 --> 00:02:15.148
So a TPU, or Tensor Processing Unit,
is Google's accelerator,

00:02:15.148 --> 00:02:17.619
custom-built for machine learning.

00:02:17.619 --> 00:02:22.368
And it enables some super fast training 
of advanced machine learning models.

00:02:22.368 --> 00:02:24.636
I'll post some resources 
down in the description

00:02:24.636 --> 00:02:26.971
for those who are interested 
in learning more.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.820
On the left-hand side of your notebook,
there's an often missed menu

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.029
which gives you access to three areas:

00:02:33.029 --> 00:02:36.660
the <i>Table of contents, 
Code snippets,</i> and <i>Files.</i>

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.943
Let's take a look 
at the Table of Contents first.

00:02:39.943 --> 00:02:43.368
If you don't already keep 
your notebook cells nicely annotated,

00:02:43.368 --> 00:02:45.751
now there's a nice incentive to do so.

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:48.542
The Table of Contents displays 
these handy headers

00:02:48.542 --> 00:02:51.477
that are clickable to help you navigate 
through your notebook

00:02:51.477 --> 00:02:53.724
and keep track of its overall structure.

00:02:54.292 --> 00:02:57.333
Next up in the menu are the code snippets.

00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:01.895
These are an absolute goldmine
of useful samples that you can use

00:03:01.895 --> 00:03:04.230
from uploading and downloading files,

00:03:04.238 --> 00:03:08.952
to visualizations, and even capturing 
images with the camera.

00:03:08.952 --> 00:03:13.616
Best of all, each snippet 
links out to a full Colab notebook

00:03:13.616 --> 00:03:17.194
where you can actually try the code
in its own environment

00:03:17.194 --> 00:03:19.618
before copying it into your own.

00:03:19.942 --> 00:03:22.969
Finally, a handy-dandy file browser

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.327
is there to help you
keep track of your files

00:03:25.327 --> 00:03:27.893
so you don't need 
to keep running bang LS (<i>!ls</i>)

00:03:27.893 --> 00:03:30.366
to see what is saved on that machine.

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:34.579
Learning data science 
and machine learning is hard enough as is.

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:38.313
So skip the hassle of installing, 
setting up, and maintaining

00:03:38.313 --> 00:03:40.880
your own environment by using Colab.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.643
Thanks for watching this episode
of <i>Cloud AI Adventures,</i>

00:03:44.643 --> 00:03:47.733
and if you enjoyed it,
please be sure to like and subscribe

00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:51.224
to get all the latest episodes 
right when they come out.

00:03:51.224 --> 00:03:55.555
For now, head over to Colab
to get your code running right now.

00:03:56.504 --> 00:03:58.654
♪ (music) ♪

